  "summary": "The health-tech landscape is showing significant momentum in AI adoption and rare disease treatments, though implementation challenges persist. Gene therapy approvals are accelerating with Ultragenyx's Sanfilippo treatment and Scholar Rock's neuromuscular disease drug reaching patients, while large pharma is making substantial bets on emerging technologies—Novartis paid $125 million for brain-delivery technology and GSK committed $750 million to Chimagen's myeloma program. On the digital health front, virtual care platforms are facing skepticism as new research shows virtual chronic kidney disease solutions don't slow disease progression or reduce costs, and hospitals are increasingly turning back to traditional Medicare as managed care denial rates rise. AI governance and oversight are becoming urgent priorities, with concerns about \"shadow AI\" in hospitals and the need for stronger scrutiny of AI agents in clinical workflows, even as healthcare organizations struggle with implementation readiness despite accelerating adoption rates. Separately, the Jehovah's Witnesses have partially eased their blood transfusion prohibition to allow blood-derived products, a significant shift with potential clinical implications.",
